## Review.cpp — Maintainability Problems

The file is 14 lines with two classes (`CsvExporter`, `TsvExporter`) whose `quote` methods are near-identical. The only difference is the delimiter character they check for.

### Duplication

| Line | Class | Delimiter | Logic |
|------|-------|-----------|-------|
| 3–6  | `CsvExporter::quote` | `,` | If field contains delimiter → wrap in `"` |
| 10–13| `TsvExporter::quote` | `\t` | If field contains delimiter → wrap in `"` |

The entire conditional + quoting body is duplicated — only the delimiter literal changes.

### What happens when this grows

- Adding a new exporter (e.g. `SsvExporter` for semicolon-separated) means copying the same three-line block again.
- Any fix to the quoting logic (e.g. also quoting fields that contain `"` or newlines, per RFC 4180) must be applied in every copy.
- Adding a new delimiter variant is a one-character change buried in a copy-paste block — easy to miss.

### Where the shared logic should live

The quoting logic belongs in a **single parameterized function** that takes the delimiter as a parameter. Two natural homes:

1. **A free function** (simplest):
   ```cpp
   std::string quote_if_needed(const std::string& field, char delimiter) {
       if (field.find(delimiter) != std::string::npos) return "\"" + field + "\"";
       return field;
   }
   ```
   Then `CsvExporter::quote` becomes `return quote_if_needed(f, ',');` and `TsvExporter::quote` becomes `return quote_if_needed(f, '\t');`.

2. **A base class** (if the exporters will grow more shared behavior):
   ```cpp
   class DelimitedExporter {
   protected:
       char delimiter_;
       std::string quote(const std::string& f) {
           if (f.find(delimiter_) != std::string::npos) return "\"" + f + "\"";
           return f;
       }
   };
   class CsvExporter : public DelimitedExporter { /* delimiter_ = ',' */ };
   class TsvExporter : public DelimitedExporter { /* delimiter_ = '\t' */ };
   ```

Option 1 is the right call here — the classes are trivial and adding inheritance for a single shared method is over-engineering. A free function eliminates the duplication with zero ceremony.

